# LF everywhere — in the repository and in the working tree.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` on a Windows checkout was writing CRLF into the working
# tree while the repository held LF. That is not cosmetic here; it broke two
# things, and the second one badly.
#
#   * `npm run lint` failed repo-wide. Prettier compares against LF and saw
#     every file as misformatted, including files no branch had touched — so
#     the signal was useless and the obvious fix, `npm run format`, would have
#     rewritten ~55 files and buried whatever diff was actually under review.
#
#   * `sqlx::migrate!` embeds each migration's checksum at **compile** time. A
#     migration written with LF, applied, then re-checked-out as CRLF produced
#     a different checksum on the next build — for a file git considered
#     unchanged. Migrations stopped applying, and the failure presented as
#     schema corruption rather than as a line-ending artifact, which is the
#     expensive kind of wrong.
#
# Nothing in the repository has ever held CRLF: `git ls-files --eol` reported
# zero such files in the index when this was added, against 141 of 173 in the
# working tree. So this file changes no committed content. It stops the
# checkout from introducing a difference that was never there.
#
# `text=auto` still lets git detect binaries and leave them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf
